Source 1

URL	https://diabetes.org/living-with-diabetes/treatment-care/diabetes-technology-guide
Site Author	American Diabetes Association (ADA)
Abstract	
The American Diabetes Association's technology guide introduces several tools used in diabetes care, including glucose meters, continuous glucose monitors, insulin pumps, and smart insulin pens. It explains how connected devices can send information to smartphone applications and help users review glucose readings, insulin doses, and other diabetes-related data. The guide describes how smart insulin pens can record doses and share information with an app, while connected glucose and insulin technologies can help users and care teams understand patterns. This source is useful for learning how digital tools fit together rather than viewing each device separately. It also helps readers compare the general purposes of different types of diabetes technology.

Source 2

URL	https://telehealth.hhs.gov/providers/best-practice-guides/telehealth-diabetes-care/telehealth-technology-diabetes-care
Site Author	U.S. Department of Health and Human Services (HHS), Telehealth.HHS.gov
Abstract	
The U.S. Department of Health and Human Services explains how telehealth and digital tools can support diabetes care. The page describes technologies such as patient messaging, online health portals, glucose meters, continuous glucose monitors, insulin pumps, and smartphone applications. It notes that apps may help people record glucose readings, insulin doses, food, and physical activity, while connected devices can make information available for review by health professionals. The article also discusses how remote communication and digital records can support education and self-management. This source is relevant to type 1 diabetes technology because it shows how information collected by devices and apps may be used beyond the moment a reading is taken, including during communication with a care team.
